#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147148 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb8b97e4-704c-5b13-97c7-6eb97aca392f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Total Given a Part and a Multiple of Ten Percent"}] :question/id #uuid "e650884c-e858-5213-8ff4-a39f9358edc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13066" :question/problem "If $0$ then what is the missing number?" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the part is $36$, the percentage is $50\\%$, and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $50\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{50}{100} = \\dfrac{50\\div 50}{100\\div 50} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nwhole &= 36 \\div 50% \\\\\n&= 36 \\div \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot \\frac{2}{1} \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147149 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147576 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c69dea-ec5d-525d-ae0f-da30e342fe63" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Total Given a Part and a Percentage"}] :question/id #uuid "4bfda07b-18fd-541a-a627-ef7f1e47f41a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13067" :question/problem "$34\\%$ of a number is $170$. What is the number?" :question/worked-solution "We're given that the part is $170$, the percentage is $34\\%$, and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $34\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{34}{100} = \\dfrac{34\\div 2}{100\\div 2} = \\dfrac{17}{50}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nwhole &= 170 \\div 34% \\\\\n&= 170 \\div \\frac{17}{50} \\\\\n&= 170 \\cdot \\frac{50}{17} \\\\\n&= \\frac{170}{17} \\cdot 50 \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot 50 \\\\\n&= 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147150 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1670b7f6-fdee-5358-b145-9254e2a4ba50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Total Given a Part and a Percentage: Word Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "bd7aaf49-0da5-53c8-a5ee-863523548957" :question/math-academy-id "e-13068" :question/problem "In the math club, there are $16$ girls and some boys. If the girls represent $40\\%$ of the total members of the club, how many members does the math club have?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a percentage, and we need to find the whole.\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of girls in the math club, which is $16$. The percentage is $40\\%$, and the whole is the total number of club members.\n\nTo find the whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $40\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{100} = \\dfrac{40\\div 20}{100\\div 20} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nwhole &= 16 \\div 40% \\\\\n&= 16 \\div \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{2} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the club has $40$ members."}]}
